course: everyday
lesson: 248
title: Soccer, American football and baseball
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The word "football" means different games in different places: soccer in the UK, Ireland and most of the world, American football in the USA and Canada, and often Australian rules or rugby league in Australia.
explain: Every sport has rules and safety gear: shin pads in soccer, a helmet and pads in tackle football, and a batting helmet in baseball.
explain: The referee's word is final: a yellow card is a warning, a red card sends a player off, and arguing can earn you a card.
explain: Some dangers are big: a soccer goal that is not fixed to the ground can fall and crush a child, and a thrown bat can hit people.
explain: A boy in London tells his friend in Chicago that he loves football, and the friend imagines helmets and touchdowns, until they work out the boy means soccer.
fact: In the UK, Ireland and most of the world, football means soccer.
fact: In the USA and Canada, football means American football (Canadian football in Canada).
fact: Shin pads are worn under the socks at practice and matches, because kicks can hurt or break the shin.
fact: You accept the referee's decision without arguing; arguing can earn a yellow card.
fact: A yellow card is a warning, and a red card means the player is sent off; a second yellow card in the same match also means a red card.
fact: An attacker is offside if, when the ball is passed forward to him, he is nearer the goal than the last defender (not counting the goalkeeper) and the ball.
fact: When a player is hurt, play stops and the referee and coach help; you can kick the ball out of play.
fact: Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal; tell an adult if it wobbles, because loose goals can fall and crush children.
fact: In tackle football players wear a helmet, pads and a mouthguard and tackle with the head up, because tackling with the head down can injure the neck.
fact: A touchdown is worth six points, followed by a try for an extra point.
fact: In baseball and softball the batter wears a helmet and drops the bat after hitting, never throwing it.
fact: Players waiting to bat stand behind the fence or in the dugout (the team's low shelter beside the field).
fact: In baseball three strikes is out, and four balls (throws outside the strike zone, the area the batter should swing at) means the batter walks to first base.
exercise: A girl from Dublin says "football". Which game does she mean? | Soccer | American football | Baseball | Basketball
exercise: A boy from Ohio says "football". Which game does he probably mean? | American football | Soccer | Rugby union | Cricket
exercise: Where do you wear shin pads in soccer? | Under your socks | Over your knees | On your elbows | Inside your shoes
exercise: The referee gives a free kick against you. What do you do? | Accept it and do not argue | Argue until the referee changes it | Kick the ball away | Walk off the pitch
exercise: What does a red card mean? | The player is sent off | A free warning | A goal for the other team | A short drinks break
exercise: What does a yellow card mean? | A warning | The player is sent off | The match is over | A penalty goal
exercise: When is an attacker offside? | When nearer the goal than the last defender as the ball is passed forward | Whenever he is standing anywhere in the other team's half | Whenever he runs faster than the defender with the ball | When he passes the ball backwards to the goalkeeper
exercise: A player is lying hurt on the pitch and you have the ball. What can you do? | Kick the ball out of play so he can be helped | Run on and score quickly while their team is one player down | Keep passing it around your team | Shout at him to get up and stop pretending
exercise: Some children are swinging on the crossbar of a goal. Why is this dangerous? | A goal not fixed down can fall and crush them | The net will tear | The goal will slowly bend into a new shape under their weight | The grass under the goal will die
exercise: What gear do you wear for tackle football? | A helmet, pads and a mouthguard | Only shin pads under your socks | Only a cap | Nothing special, just your normal clothes
exercise: How should you tackle in American football? | With your head up | Leading with the top of your helmet | With your eyes closed | By grabbing the face mask
exercise: How many points is a touchdown worth? | Six | Three | One | Ten
exercise: You hit the baseball. What do you do with the bat? | Drop it and run | Throw it behind you | Throw it towards first base | Carry it to first base
exercise: Where do you wait for your turn to bat? | Behind the fence or in the dugout | Right next to the batter, so you can watch closely | Behind the catcher | On the pitcher's mound
exercise: How many strikes make a batter out? | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: A girl in Kansas swings her bat to warm up while friends stand close by. What is the danger? | The swinging bat can hit someone | The bat could get scratched on the ground | She may get too tired before her turn | The ball may roll away
test: A boy in Manchester and a boy in Boston both say "I play football on Saturdays". What is true? | They probably play different games | They play the same game | Neither plays a team sport | Football means rugby in both places
test: A batter has four balls thrown outside the strike zone. What happens? | He walks to first base | He is out | His team gets a run | He bats again from zero
test: The referee shows a player a yellow card, and he keeps shouting at her. What may happen next? | A second yellow, which means a red card | He wins a free kick for his team | The referee must apologise to him in front of everyone | Nothing, because shouting at referees is allowed
test: You notice that the goal at your park rocks when the wind blows. What should you do? | Tell an adult and keep off it | Hang on it with your friends to hold it still | Climb on top to fix it yourself | Ignore it, because goals are meant to move a bit in the wind
test: A team scores a touchdown and then the extra point. How many points is that? | Seven | Six | Three | Eight
test: Why should a young football player learn to tackle with the head up? | Tackling with the head down can injure the neck | It is easier to see the cheerleaders at the side of the field | The helmet falls off otherwise | It makes the tackle count double in the score
test: Why do soccer players wear shin pads even at practice? | A kick to the shin can hurt or break the bone | They make the legs run faster | Coaches like the colour of them on the team | Socks would just fall down round the ankles without them
test: An opponent falls and holds his knee. You are about to score. What does good sportsmanship say? | Stop play so he can be helped | Score first, help later | Pretend you did not see him | Ask him to move off the pitch
test: in the uk my cousin says football, does he mean the helmet one? | No, in the UK football means soccer | Yes, football always means helmets | Yes, it means rugby too | It means baseball in the UK
test: ref gave a penalty that was SO wrong, gonna tell him | Accept it, arguing can get you a yellow card | Shout at him till he changes his mind | Push him a little | Walk off the pitch n take the ball with u
test: forgot my shin pads, can i still play, it's just practice | No, wear shin pads at practice too | Yes, practice kicks don't hurt | Yes, if you play in goal | Pull your socks up higher instead, that's the same
test: hit the ball!! do i throw the bat n run? | No, drop the bat and run | Yes, throw it to the side | Take the bat with you | Throw it at the catcher
explain: A boy in Ohio played soccer without shin pads; a hard tackle cracked his shin bone and he was in a cast for six weeks.
explain: A high-school football player in Texas kept playing after a big hit to the head because he felt "okay"; a second hit made it much worse, and he was off school for weeks.
fact: A concussion (a brain injury from a knock to the head) can happen without being knocked out, and playing on after a head injury can make it much more serious.
fact: Shin pads, helmets and mouth guards stop many broken bones and broken teeth.
test: forgot my shin pads, just play without | Don't, a kick can break your shin | Fine, pads only slow you down anyway | Fine if you don't tackle | Wear two pairs of socks, that's just as good
test: took a big hit to the head, feel kinda off but wanna finish the game | Stop and tell the coach, a head injury needs checking | Finish the game first, then have a rest | Keep playing if you can stand | Have a drink n carry on
test: Why must a player with a head knock not go back on the same day? | A second hit can make the brain injury much worse | They might be too tired to play well | The rules say only one go | It is unfair to the other team to have a player who might faint
test: did u hear a kid was swinging on the goal post and the whole goal fell on him? real lesson? | Never climb or hang on a goal; tell an adult if it wobbles | Only swing on goals that look heavy | Goals are bolted down so they're always safe | Swing from the side, not the bar
test: apparently a player got a bump on the head, said he was fine, kept playing and ended up in hospital for weeks. what's the true lesson? | Stop playing after a head injury; concussion can worsen | Keep playing as long as you weren't knocked out | Head bumps only matter if you bleed | Drink some water and carry on, it'll clear
test: my mate says in baseball u throw the bat after hitting to run faster. right? | No, drop the bat; throwing it can hurt people | Yes, it helps you run | Yes, throw it hard toward first base | Yes, as long as nobody is standing anywhere near you
exam: Identify the meaning of a yellow card in soccer. | It is a warning to the player | It means the player is sent off | It awards a goal to the other side | It signals the end of the match
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises concussion? | It may occur without loss of consciousness | It occurs only if one is knocked out | It is cured by continuing to play | It affects only adult players and never children
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice regarding shin pads. | They are worn under the socks at practice and matches | They are worn only in professional matches | They are worn over the boots | They are optional in tackling games
exam: Ascertain the number of points awarded for a touchdown in American football. | Six points | Three points | One point | Ten points
